# phpseclib's ASN.1 decoder accepted object identifiers up to 4096 bytes,
# so a certificate carrying near-4KB OIDs drove outsized parsing work in
# every chain-verification consumer (resource exhaustion through an
# over-generous cap, CWE-400/CWE-770). _decodeOID in
# phpseclib/File/ASN1.php measures the element with `$len =
# strlen($content)`, rejects above 4096, then walks the bytes one at a
# time accumulating each into a growing Math_BigInteger --
# `$n = $n->bitwise_leftShift(7); $n = $n->bitwise_or(new
# Math_BigInteger($temp & 0x7F))` -- so every admitted byte costs work
# proportional to everything accumulated before it. The cap is what sets
# the amplification ceiling. Fix d53d2021bc ("ASN1: reduce length of
# supported OIDs from 4096 bytes to 128 bytes", citing OpenJDK's
# ObjectIdentifier limit) changes only the constant: `if ($len > 128)`.
#
# ## Re-measured at the current tip
#
# Three `--cache off` runs per revision (timeout 180, --max-ram 6GB) over
# the whole checkout: 108 findings at both revisions, identical across
# all six runs. The only finding on ASN1.php is the same VYQL-RF-003 row
# at :1147 on both sides (code.ExternalEntryInput from _encode_der's
# $mapping into a code.HtmlRender sink), untouched by the fix. The
# `-flags only` view reports 56 flags, none of them a DOS flag and the
# only one on ASN1.php a neutralising core.PathAccessCheck at :1453, so
# the zero is real and not a `-flags` replacement misread. `vyql match`
# on a minimal reproduction of the function labels nothing at all: no
# source, no sink, no control.
#
# ## Why no shipped rule reaches it
#
# No rule in the dos pack has a PHP feeder: every one of the 45 is bound
# to another language's shape. VYQL-DOS-029, the parser-loop default this
# family pins against, is fed only by bindings/c/mce54b166.vyql on the C
# frontend fact `numeric_parser.missing_progress_check`, and
# VYQL-DOS-050's `loop_restart_without_progress` is a JavaScript frontend
# fact. Nothing minted since the first review names a cap magnitude.
#
# ## What a binding would have to key on
#
# A generic shape binding -- a strlen-measured cap, a per-byte `ord`
# walk, an accumulating big-integer shift -- was probed as a throwaway
# binding on both revisions: it labels the function on each side, so it
# does not discriminate, and it reports nothing on two unrelated PHP
# trees (67 and 574 files). The two revisions differ only in the literal
# 4096 versus 128 and the same number inside a comment, so the only
# formulation that separates them names that literal; the discriminating
# probe does land exactly on ASN1.php:1274 at the parent, is silent at
# the fix, and reports nothing on the two other trees, but the constant
# is the entire diff -- a binding rejecting 4096 is the fixing commit's
# own choice, not a property of the weakness, and the next variant with
# an 8192-byte cap walks past it. The reference point is protocol
# knowledge (OpenJDK's 128-byte ObjectIdentifier limit), which no
# construct-level predicate carries. The nearest consumable concept,
# code.NumericParserMissingProgressCheck, also describes the wrong
# defect: this loop advances its cursor unconditionally every iteration
# and terminates, where that rule is about a parse position that fails to
# advance.
#
# Pin both revisions as a double miss. When vocabulary that can ask
# whether a decoder's size cap is within the range its own format
# documents lands, the first block must flip to expect.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-vocabulary-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1572: the oid decoder caps at four kilobytes and accumulates big integers per byte"
  lang php
  file phpseclib/File/ASN1.php
  reject VYQL-DOS-050
  reject VYQL-DOS-029
  code
  ```
  <?php

  class File_ASN1
  {
      function _decodeOID($content)
      {
          $oid = array();
          $pos = 0;
          $len = strlen($content);
          // see https://github.com/openjdk/jdk/blob/2deb318c9f047ec5a4b160d66a4b52f93688ec42/src/java.base/share/classes/sun/security/util/ObjectIdentifier.java#L55
          if ($len > 4096) {
              //user_error('Object Identifier size is limited to 4096 bytes');
              return false;
          }

          if (ord($content[$len - 1]) & 0x80) {
              return false;
          }

          $n = new Math_BigInteger();
          while ($pos < $len) {
              $temp = ord($content[$pos++]);
              $n = $n->bitwise_leftShift(7);
              $n = $n->bitwise_or(new Math_BigInteger($temp & 0x7F));
              if (~$temp & 0x80) {
                  $oid[] = $n;
                  $n = new Math_BigInteger();
              }
          }

          return implode('.', $oid);
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1572: the fixed decoder caps at the jdk objectidentifier limit"
  lang php
  file phpseclib/File/ASN1.php
  reject VYQL-DOS-050
  reject VYQL-DOS-029
  code
  ```
  <?php

  class File_ASN1
  {
      function _decodeOID($content)
      {
          $oid = array();
          $pos = 0;
          $len = strlen($content);
          // see https://github.com/openjdk/jdk/blob/2deb318c9f047ec5a4b160d66a4b52f93688ec42/src/java.base/share/classes/sun/security/util/ObjectIdentifier.java#L55
          if ($len > 128) {
              //user_error('Object Identifier size is limited to 128 bytes');
              return false;
          }

          if (ord($content[$len - 1]) & 0x80) {
              return false;
          }

          $n = new Math_BigInteger();
          while ($pos < $len) {
              $temp = ord($content[$pos++]);
              $n = $n->bitwise_leftShift(7);
              $n = $n->bitwise_or(new Math_BigInteger($temp & 0x7F));
              if (~$temp & 0x80) {
                  $oid[] = $n;
                  $n = new Math_BigInteger();
              }
          }

          return implode('.', $oid);
      }
  }
  ```
